#!/bin/sh
#
# Readahead for the microSD root: 64 KB.
#
# RE-MEASURED 2026-09-12 and CHANGED 128 -> 64. The 128 KB choice below was
# made judging stalls with a frame instrument that was reading
# CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID - lvdesk's own CPU time - rather than wall clock, so
# it could not see frame pacing at all. That evidence is void.
#
# Re-run against what the dips actually track: prboom's major faults and the
# card traffic those faults cause, 60 s of play per arm, one boot, kernel #198:
#
#     readahead   major faults   sectors read
#        16 kB        314           15,336   (7.5 MB)
#        32 kB        141            8,968   (4.4 MB)
#        64 kB        101           10,296   (5.0 MB)
#       128 kB        120           19,296   (9.4 MB)
#       256 kB         97           24,712   (12.1 MB)
#
# 128 kB is beaten on BOTH axes by both 32 and 64. Large readahead buys
# slightly fewer faults by dragging in far more data, and on a board whose
# binding constraint is memory that extra data evicts the pages whose absence
# caused the faults. 64 kB is the knee: 101 faults for 5.0 MB against 120 for
# 9.4 MB.
#
# 256 kB has the fewest faults and reads 2.4x the data to get there. If a
# future workload is bandwidth-rich and memory-rich, that trade changes.
#
# The original 128 KB reasoning is kept below for the history of the trade.
#
# It was 512 KB, which measured slightly better for a sequential read (11.8
# against 8.3 MB/s on a 4 MB file, 2026-09-11). But the card's real workload
# is RANDOM: a game re-reading WAD lumps, swap, page-cache refaults. Under a
# Doom timedemo the 512 KB window turned every small miss into a
# half-megabyte read - 98 MB from the card in 280 s, 2.2 MB pushed to swap
# to make room for it - against 5.4 MB read and 148 KB swapped at 16 KB,
# with the frame rate a little better rather than worse. The streaming case
# on this board is Wi-Fi-bound long before 8 MB/s matters. Set per-device
# because it is a queue attribute, not a sysctl.
#
# RAISED 16 -> 128 KB on 2026-09-12, chasing reported hitching in play. The
# hitches are major faults: prboom mmaps the WAD and holds only ~56 KB of
# 4 MB resident, so every new texture, sprite or sound is an SD read, and
# they arrive in bursts - 41 faults and 1.5 MB inside 1.7 s was sampled
# under the attract demo. Major faults per 30 s of demo, cold cache each
# arm, baseline repeated three times for a noise floor of about +-3%:
#
#     16 KB   107, 109, 113        512 KB    69
#     64 KB    65                 1024 KB   336   <- the cliff is real
#    128 KB    55                  256 KB   42, 48, 61
#
# Over a 120 s window: 272 faults / 601 KB read / 916 KB swapped at 16 KB
# against 193 / 1099 / 1248 at 128 KB. So ~29% fewer stalls for half a
# megabyte more reading and a third of a megabyte more swap over two
# minutes. 256 KB measured slightly better still and is NOT taken: 512
# already regresses and 1024 is catastrophic, so 128 keeps a wide margin
# from a cliff whose position will move with memory pressure.
#
# The 98 MB figure above stands for what it measured - a full timedemo at
# 512 KB - and did not reproduce at 128 KB over two minutes. Both numbers
# are kept because they were taken under different loads, and the cliff
# above shows why the difference matters.
#
# This does NOT cure the hitching, and no readahead can: the WAD is 4 MB
# and free memory is ~3 MB, so the page cache can never hold it. The two
# things that would are prboom's binary in XIP (it runs from the card
# today, 244 KB resident competing for the same cache) and the WAD itself
# in XIP flash, which needs a repartition - xip1 has 790 KB spare.

case "$1" in
	start)
		for q in /sys/block/mmcblk*/queue; do
			[ -w "$q/read_ahead_kb" ] && echo 64 > "$q/read_ahead_kb"
		done
		;;
	stop|restart|reload)
		;;
esac
exit 0
